{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: wider side gutters on larger screens so page content is not
  edge-to-edge on laptops and monitors. Mobile (16px) and tablet (40px) gutters
  are left as Horizon ships them, and the safe-area insets are preserved at
  every breakpoint. This renders after base.css, so it overrides the default
  --page-margin at equal specificity.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  @media screen and (min-width: 1000px) {
    .page-width-wide,
    .page-width-normal,
    .page-width-narrow,
    .page-width-content {
      --page-margin: max(64px, var(--safe-area-inset-left, 0px), var(--safe-area-inset-right, 0px));
    }
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 1400px) {
    .page-width-wide,
    .page-width-normal,
    .page-width-narrow,
    .page-width-content {
      --page-margin: max(80px, var(--safe-area-inset-left, 0px), var(--safe-area-inset-right, 0px));
    }
  }
{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: slideshow arrows. On desktop Horizon only reveals them on hover
  or keyboard focus (opacity:0 in snippets/slideshow-arrows.liquid, revealed by
  the arrowsSlideIn animation on slideshow-container[animate-arrows]:hover in
  base.css); these rules keep the chevrons permanently visible and inset them
  further from the slide edge than the theme's own var(--padding-xs). On phones
  they are hidden altogether in favour of swiping - see the media query below.

  Class is repeated to raise specificity above the theme's rules without
  !important: the hover rule is (0,3,2) and the margin rule (0,3,1).
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-arrows .slideshow-control {
    opacity: 1;
    animation: none;
  }

  /* Pushed to the far edges of the carousel. slideshow-container is
     overflow-x: clip, so 0 is as far out as they can go - a negative margin
     would just be clipped. This replaces the theme's var(--padding-xs) inset. */
  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-arrows .slideshow-control:first-of-type {
    margin-inline-start: 0;
  }

  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-arrows .slideshow-control:last-of-type {
    margin-inline-end: 0;
  }

  /* Phones advance the carousel by swiping (slideshow-slides is overflow-x
     scroll with scroll-snap), and the dots show position, so the arrows are
     redundant there and only crowd the slide. display:none keeps the buttons in
     the DOM so the component's previous/next refs still resolve. */
  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-arrows {
      display: none;
    }
  }

{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: hero carousel sizing.

  FULL VIEWPORT HEIGHT AT EVERY WIDTH. The slideshow section offers only
  auto/small/medium/large slide heights with no full-screen option. min-height
  (never height) means a slide whose content does not fit still grows rather
  than clipping - that combination is what previously hid the mobile CTAs.

  CLIPPING FIX (all widths): blocks/_slide.liquid sets
  .slide__content { height: 100% } and slideshow-slide is overflow:hidden. Once
  the slide has a definite height that locks the content box to it and anything
  taller is cut off. height:auto + min-height:100% lets the content fill the
  slide and push it taller when needed, so nothing is ever hidden.

  svh rather than vh so mobile browsers measure the visible area; the vh line is
  the fallback. --header-group-height is subtracted so the slide fills the space
  below the sticky header; it is set on document.body before first paint in
  layout/theme.liquid and maintained by header.js.

  MOBILE TYPE: base.css sizes each preset from var(--font-<preset>--size), so
  redefining those variables on .slide__content scales the slide's type down
  without fighting selector specificity or touching the global settings.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide {
    min-height: calc(100vh - var(--header-group-height, 0px));
    min-height: calc(100svh - var(--header-group-height, 0px));
    max-height: none;
  }

  /* Content fills the slide but is never capped by it. */
  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide > .slide__content {
    height: auto;
    min-height: 100%;
  }

  /* Centre the content in the tall slide. .slide__content is a plain block
     container, so the theme's `.slide__content > * { margin: auto }` can only
     centre horizontally - vertical auto margins compute to 0 in block layout,
     which left everything stacked at the top of a 100svh slide with a large
     empty gap beneath. Making it a flex column lets those auto margins resolve
     vertically. margin-inline is zeroed and width forced so the row keeps its
     full width instead of shrink-wrapping. */
  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide > .slide__content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide > .slide__content > * {
    width: 100%;
    margin-inline: 0;
  }

  /* Noto Nastaliq Urdu, self-hosted from assets/ so it is served by the Shopify
     CDN rather than fonts.gstatic.com (theme check RemoteAsset, and one less
     third-party connection above the fold). Subset to only the glyphs in
     "خوش آمدید" via the Google Fonts text= parameter: 23KB instead of the
     ~200KB full Nastaliq face. The unicode-range is Google's own, so the
     browser only downloads it when those characters are actually used. */
  @font-face {
    font-family: 'Noto Nastaliq Urdu';
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-display: swap;
    src: url({{ 'noto-nastaliq-urdu-subset.woff2' | asset_url }}) format('woff2');
    unicode-range: U+20, U+622, U+627, U+62e-62f, U+634, U+645, U+648, U+653, U+6cc;
  }

  /* Urdu eyebrow. The accent font (Lora) has no Arabic glyphs, so without this
     the browser picks an arbitrary fallback. Nastaliq faces first, then Naskh,
     then the Latin accent font; the stack is per-glyph, so whichever is present
     renders the Urdu. Nastaliq is a tall, steeply sloped script that collides
     with itself at normal leading, hence the roomier line-height and padding.
     align-self centres the pill without stretching it - the block keeps
     width: fit-content, so .text-block--background stays a pill rather than
     becoming a full-width bar. */
  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide .slide__content .text-block--background,
  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide .slide__content .text-block--background > * {
    font-family: 'Noto Nastaliq Urdu', 'Jameel Noori Nastaleeq', 'Urdu Typesetting',
      'Noto Naskh Arabic', 'Geeza Pro', var(--font-accent--family), serif;
  }

  .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide .slide__content .text-block--background {
    /* The theme reads these two custom properties (.custom-font-size and
       .custom-typography in base.css), so overriding the variables beats
       redeclaring font-size / line-height against inline styles. Nastaliq needs
       both more size and far more leading than Latin type: the script slopes
       steeply and its descenders run deep, so at the block's own 1.125rem/2 it
       renders cramped and clips inside the pill. */
    --font-size: 1.625rem;
    --line-height: 2.2;
    align-self: center;
    padding-block-start: 6px;
    padding-block-end: 14px;
    padding-inline-start: 26px;
    padding-inline-end: 26px;
  }


  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    /* The content panel is full-width and ~90% opaque on a phone, so it would
       cover the slide's background image completely. Pin the panel to the
       bottom of the full-height slide: the photo shows through the upper part
       and the text still sits on an opaque panel.
       justify-content alone is not enough - the theme's
       `.slide__content > * { margin: auto }` would re-centre it, so the block
       margins are zeroed here. */
    .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide > .slide__content {
      justify-content: flex-end;
    }

    .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide > .slide__content > * {
      margin-block: 0;
    }

    /* The Urdu eyebrow shows on phones too. Nastaliq is scaled back a little so
       the pill sits comfortably in the narrower panel - the desktop rule above
       sets 1.625rem, and this media query comes later in the file so it wins at
       equal specificity. Padding is trimmed to match the smaller glyphs while
       still clearing Nastaliq's deep descenders. */
    .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide .slide__content .text-block--background {
      --font-size: 1.25rem;
      --line-height: 2.1;
      padding-block-start: 4px;
      padding-block-end: 10px;
      padding-inline-start: 20px;
      padding-inline-end: 20px;
    }

    /* Smaller type so the whole slide fits one screen. */
    .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide .slide__content {
      --font-h1--size: 1.75rem;
      --font-h2--size: 1.5rem;
      --font-paragraph--size: 0.9375rem;
      --font-h4--size: 1.0625rem;
      --font-h5--size: 0.9375rem;
    }

    /* Tighter inner padding. spacing-style only sets custom properties inline,
       so the real padding declarations can still be overridden here.

       The bottom value clears the pagination dots: sections/slideshow.liquid
       hardcodes controls_on_media: true, so slideshow-controls is
       position:absolute / bottom:0 and overlays the slide at
       min-height: var(--minimum-touch-target) = 44px. 60px leaves the dots their
       44px plus a 16px gap, so the CTAs are never underneath them. */
    .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide > .slide__content {
      padding-inline-start: 20px;
      padding-inline-end: 20px;
      padding-block-start: 24px;
      padding-block-end: 60px;
    }

    /* Keep the stack compact on small screens. */
    .slideshow-section.slideshow-section slideshow-slide .slide__content .group-block-content {
      gap: 14px;
    }
  }
{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: header menu hover + icon affordances.

  Menu links are distinguished by colour and weight rather than decoration:
  muted at rest, near-black and thickened on hover, near-black and semibold for
  the current page.

  Starting from a muted resting colour is what makes the change readable - navy
  on hover against the #17202A default would have been almost invisible, since
  both sit above 16:1 on white and differ barely at all from each other.

  Weight is doing the work colour alone could not. The usable range is narrow:
  resting links still have to pass AA, so they cannot go lighter than about
  5:1 (#667085), which caps how far the colour can travel. Gold is no help -
  brand #C9A24A is 2.4:1 as text, and deepening it far enough to pass turns it
  muddy brown rather than highlighted, so it stays on backgrounds and accents.

  Selectors carry an extra class to clear Horizon's own hover rules, which run at
  (0,2,0) for the subdue-the-rest rule and (0,3,0) for the restore-hovered rule.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  /* resting: muted, so the active and hovered states have somewhere to travel */
  .header .menu-list .menu-list__link {
    color: #667085;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }

  /* hover / keyboard focus: near-black plus a faux-bold. text-shadow thickens
     the glyphs without changing their metrics - a real font-weight change here
     would re-measure the text and shunt the whole nav sideways on every hover. */
  .header .menu-list .menu-list__list-item:hover .menu-list__link,
  .header .menu-list .menu-list__list-item:focus-within .menu-list__link {
    color: #0B1F33;
    text-shadow: 0 0 0.45px currentColor;
  }

  /* current page: real semibold. Safe here because it is applied once at load
     rather than during interaction, so there is no visible shift. */
  .header .menu-list .menu-list__link--active {
    color: #0B1F33;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-shadow: none;
  }

  /* Search / account / cart: soft champagne disc on hover. The icon itself stays
     dark so contrast holds; only the backing tint carries the brand colour. */
  .header .header-actions__action {
    border-radius: 50%;
    transition: background-color 0.2s ease, color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .header .header-actions__action:hover,
  .header .header-actions__action:focus-visible {
    background-color: #E5C8783D;
    color: #0B1F33;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .header .menu-list__link,
    .header .header-actions__action {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: value strip layout and node connectors.

  The four value nodes are generic `group` blocks in templates/index.json, so
  the medallion and the label are already merchant-editable there - the disc's
  fill, gold ring, radius and padding are all group settings, and the icon size
  and colour are icon-block settings. What settings cannot express is the
  responsive column count and a line drawn between nodes, so that part lives
  here.

  Scoped by section id because the strip is composed entirely from generic
  blocks and has no component class of its own. `value_strip` is the template
  key in templates/index.json and is stable for the same reason the block ids
  are: saved merchant configuration depends on it.

  Matched on the end of the id rather than the whole of it. Shopify does not
  render a JSON template's section as `shopify-section-value_strip`; it
  prefixes the key with the template's own generated id, so the element is
  `shopify-section-template--27862213722339__value_strip` on this store and a
  different number on any other. Only the `__value_strip` suffix is ours to
  rely on. Pairing the attribute selector with .shopify-section also keeps
  these rules above Horizon's own (0,2,0) and (0,3,0) layout rules for
  .layout-panel-flex--row and its children. If the section is ever duplicated
  or re-keyed, these rules need the new key.

  LAYOUT. From 990px the four nodes sit on one line, which is the section's own
  `row` direction, so nothing here touches it. Below that they wrap to a 2x2
  grid. Horizon already sets flex-wrap from --flex-wrap-mobile under 750px, but
  not between 750px and 989px, so wrap is set explicitly to cover both. The
  basis is half the line minus half a gutter, which is what makes two columns
  plus one gutter fill the width exactly. Row gap is widened past the column
  gutter because the nodes are much taller than they are wide, and a square gap
  reads as cramped between stacked rows.

  CONNECTORS. Each node after the first on its line draws a line back to the
  node before it. The span is measured centre to centre, which is the one
  distance CSS can state here without knowing anything about the medallion:
  100% of a node plus one gutter. Both ends are then pulled back by
  --tm-value-strip-connector-inset so the line stops short of each circle
  instead of running under it, leaving clear air between the line and the
  medallion at both ends.

  That inset is the circle's radius plus the breathing space wanted beyond it,
  so the 12px is the gap you actually see. Because the line now ends in the
  open rather than beneath an opaque disc, --tm-value-strip-node-size has to be
  right: it is the medallion's diameter, the sum of three merchant settings on
  a descendant element that CSS cannot read from here - icon width + twice the
  disc padding + twice the border width, currently 28 + 32 + 2. It sets both
  how high the line sits and where each end stops, so if those settings change
  this value needs to change with them, or the line will sit off the circle's
  centre line and crowd or overlap its edge.

  On the 2x2 the connector joins the two nodes of each row - nth-child(even) -
  and the rows are left unjoined. The third node starts a new line, so the
  `+ .group-block` form used on one line would draw a connector back across the
  full width of the strip to the node above it.

  COLOUR. Derived from --color-border-rgb. On a section with its own
  background, snippets/contrast-override.liquid re-points that token at the
  palette extreme that contrasts the background, so on the navy band the line
  inverts to a light hairline on its own. --opacity-30-60 is the theme's
  brightness-aware pair from snippets/brightness-opacities.liquid: 0.3 over a
  light background, 0.6 over a dark one, which keeps it readable on navy
  without pinning a second value here. The literal fallback covers a band with
  no background colour set, where that token is not emitted. The line is solid
  rather than faded at its ends, because the medallions already end it.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  .shopify-section[id$='__value_strip'] .section-content-wrapper {
    --tm-value-strip-node-size: 62px;
    --tm-value-strip-connector-inset: calc(var(--tm-value-strip-node-size) / 2 + 12px);
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__value_strip'] .section-content-wrapper > .group-block::before {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline-start: calc(-50% - var(--gap, 24px) + var(--tm-value-strip-connector-inset));
    inset-block-start: calc(var(--tm-value-strip-node-size) / 2);
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    inline-size: calc(100% + var(--gap, 24px) - 2 * var(--tm-value-strip-connector-inset));
    block-size: 1px;
    background-color: rgb(var(--color-border-rgb) / var(--opacity-30-60, 0.3));
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
    /* One line: every node joins back to the one before it. */
    .shopify-section[id$='__value_strip'] .section-content-wrapper > .group-block + .group-block::before {
      content: '';
    }
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 989px) {
    .shopify-section[id$='__value_strip'] .section-content-wrapper {
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      row-gap: calc(var(--gap, 24px) * 1.5);
    }

    .shopify-section[id$='__value_strip'] .section-content-wrapper > .group-block {
      flex: 0 0 calc(50% - var(--gap, 24px) / 2);
    }

    /* Second node of each row only. */
    .shopify-section[id$='__value_strip'] .section-content-wrapper > .group-block:nth-child(even)::before {
      content: '';
    }
  }
{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: footer column proportions.

  sections/footer.liquid lays the footer out as a grid of equal columns -
  `repeat(var(--grid-columns), 1fr)` - so the brand column, which carries a logo
  and a paragraph of copy, gets exactly the same width as a menu column holding
  a handful of short links. That is what made the footer read as misaligned: the
  brand copy wrapped over six short lines while the link columns sat half empty
  beside it.

  Giving the first column half again the width of the others lets the paragraph
  breathe and pulls the three link columns into an even rhythm across the rest
  of the row. Only applied to the four-column case, matched the same way the
  theme's own tablet rule does, so a footer with a different number of blocks
  keeps Horizon's equal columns.

  Desktop only. Below 990px the theme already collapses to one or two columns,
  where equal widths are correct.

  Scoped by the id suffix because the footer is a section group: the rendered id
  is `shopify-section-sections--<generated>__footer_main`, and only the
  `__footer_main` part is ours. Pairing it with .shopify-section keeps this
  above the theme's own (0,1,0) grid rule.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  /* TrendMarkaz: a visible edge where the page ends and the footer begins.

     The footer group now sits on its own warm surface, color_palette.color4,
     so it reads as one block against the white page above. Before this
     footer_main carried the page background, which on every template without
     the newsletter section (about, contact, collections, blog, the policy
     pages) meant grey links floating on white with no boundary at all. The home
     and product pages hid the problem, because the dark newsletter band
     happened to sit directly above the footer and supplied the edge for it.

     The utilities bar stays on the same colour rather than stepping a shade
     deeper, and keeps its own 1px divider to separate itself. color5 was the
     obvious step, but it puts the #667085 copyright at 4.40:1, under the 4.5:1
     AA floor; on color4 the same text sits at 4.72:1.

     The rule is still worth keeping with the new background: it holds the edge
     if a section above ever lands on a warm tone too, and it is the same border
     colour the utilities divider already uses, so the footer is bracketed by
     matching lines top and bottom.

     Scoped by the id suffix because the footer is a section group: the rendered
     id is `shopify-section-sections--<generated>__footer_main`, and only the
     `__footer_main` part is ours. */
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .section-background {
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  @media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .footer-content[style*='--grid-columns: 4'] {
      grid-template-columns: 1.6fr 1fr 1fr 1fr;
    }
  }

  /* Phones: two menu columns rather than one long stack.

     sections/footer.liquid lays the footer out as `grid-template-columns: 1fr`
     below 750px, so every block becomes a full-width row and the footer runs on
     for several screens. Two columns halves that.

     The brand block keeps the full width and centres, because it carries a logo
     and a sentence rather than a list of short links - at half width the
     sentence would wrap to five or six lines beside a column of one-word links.
     Centring it also gives the logo somewhere to sit now that it is 48px on
     mobile. :first-child is the brand because it is first in the section's
     block order; the three menu columns follow it into the two-column grid. */
  /* Up to 989px, not 749px. sections/footer.liquid already drops the footer to
     two columns from 750px, so the four-column desktop layout only exists above
     989px - which means a tablet or a landscape phone was getting the two-column
     grid with the brand still squeezed into one half and aligned left. Matching
     the theme's own breakpoint keeps the brand full width and centred wherever
     the footer is not in its desktop form. */
  @media screen and (max-width: 989px) {
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .footer-content {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }

    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .footer-content > :first-child {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .footer-content > :first-child .group-block-content {
      align-items: center;
    }

    /* The blurb is a text block at 100% width, so snippets/text.liquid writes
       its alignment setting into --text-align and applies that to the block's
       children through `.text-block > *`, which beats a text-align inherited
       from the centred column.

       That variable is written inline on the block, and an inline custom
       property beats one from a stylesheet, so re-pointing it alone leaves the
       paragraph reading `start`. The children are therefore matched as well as
       the block and given text-align directly, which is what actually moves the
       text. The merchant's desktop alignment setting is untouched. */
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .footer-content > :first-child .text-block,
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .footer-content > :first-child .text-block > * {
      --text-align: center;
      text-align: center;
    }
  }

  /* Social icons in their own brand colours.

     blocks/social-links.liquid offers one colour for the whole set, so the
     per-platform colours have to come from here. Matching on the href rather
     than the aria-label: the label is the platform name run through
     `capitalize`, while the domain is what actually identifies the service and
     cannot drift. Every platform the block supports is listed, so any account
     added later is already covered. */
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='facebook.'] {
    color: #1877f2;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='instagram.'] {
    color: #e4405f;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='youtube.'] {
    color: #ff0000;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='tiktok.'] {
    color: #010101;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='linkedin.'] {
    color: #0a66c2;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='pinterest.'] {
    color: #bd081c;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='vimeo.'] {
    color: #1ab7ea;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='bsky.'] {
    color: #0285ff;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='tumblr.'] {
    color: #36465d;
  }

  /* X and Threads are black marks. Snapchat is deliberately absent: its brand
     yellow is close to invisible on the warm footer background, so it is better
     left on the inherited text colour than given a mark nobody can see. */
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='twitter.'],
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='x.com'],
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a[href*='threads.'] {
    color: #000000;
  }

  /* The utilities row is `1fr auto 1fr` so the policies link sits centred
     between the copyright and the social icons. With no social accounts
     configured the icon wrapper still renders - empty, zero width - and holds
     its column open, which leaves the policies link stranded mid-row with
     nothing to balance it.

     Collapsing to two tracks puts it at the end of the row instead.

     The test is "the wrapper contains no icon", written as :not(:has(...))
     rather than :has(... :not(:has(*))): a :has() may not be nested inside
     another :has(), but one inside :not() is fine. :empty is no good either -
     the block emits a whitespace text node, so the wrapper is never empty.

     The moment a social account is added the wrapper gains a child, this stops
     matching, and Horizon's three-track layout returns on its own. */
  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .utilities:not(:has(> .social-icons__wrapper > *)) {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
    }
  }
{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: reviews carousel arrows.

  Horizon's slideshow arrows are built to sit on top of hero media, so
  snippets/slideshow-arrows.liquid starts them at opacity 0 and base.css only
  brings them in through the arrowsSlideIn animation while the container is
  hovered or focused within. On a review carousel that is the wrong default:
  nothing indicates the row scrolls until the pointer happens to land on it,
  and on a touch screen there is no hover at all.

  These rules pin them visible. The shape is already right for a light section
  - `circle` fills from --color-primary-button-background, which is brand navy
  with a white chevron - so only visibility and the end states need handling.

  assets/slideshow.js already sets `disabled` on each button when the carousel
  is not infinite: previous at index 0, next once the last slide is in view.
  blocks/_carousel-content.liquid passes infinite: false, so that logic is live
  and all that was missing was a visual state for it. Dimming rather than
  hiding keeps the row's width stable and keeps the affordance legible.

  Scoped by the section key so the hero slideshow and any product carousels
  keep Horizon's hover behaviour.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  .shopify-section[id$='__product_reviews'] slideshow-arrows .slideshow-control {
    opacity: 1;
    animation: none;
    transition: opacity var(--animation-speed) var(--animation-easing);
  }

  /* blocks/_carousel-content.liquid removes a disabled control outright with
     `display: none`, which leaves a hole where the arrow was and makes the pair
     jump around as you scroll. Keeping it in the layout and dimming it instead
     means the control stays where the eye expects it and simply reads as
     unavailable. `flex` is what the circle shape sets, and is what centres the
     chevron inside the button. */
  .shopify-section[id$='__product_reviews'] slideshow-arrows .slideshow-control[disabled] {
    display: flex;
    opacity: var(--opacity-30, 0.3);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Exactly `columns` cards, with no part of the next one showing.

     --peek-next-slide-size is 3rem globally and is subtracted from the width
     each card is given, which is what leaves a sliver of the fourth card at the
     edge. Zeroing it here lets three cards plus two gaps fill the content width
     exactly. The variable is read by the card width calc in
     blocks/_carousel-content.liquid, so setting it on the carousel is enough -
     it inherits down. */
  .shopify-section[id$='__product_reviews'] .carousel-content {
    --peek-next-slide-size: 0px;
  }

  /* Arrow placement differs by breakpoint.

     Horizon absolutely positions slideshow-arrows across the whole slideshow
     (`inset: 0`, `position="center"`) so they sit at the left and right edges,
     vertically centred. That is right when they float over edge-to-edge hero
     media, and it is still what we want on a phone, where one card fills the
     row and a small control at each edge reads as the obvious way to move.

     On desktop it is wrong: three cards fill the row, so an arrow at each edge
     lands on top of the first and last card. There they are pinned to the
     top-right instead - bottom at 100% of the track, so they sit in the
     header's lower padding, inset by the page margin so the trailing arrow
     lines up with the card edge rather than the viewport edge. The header's
     blocks are left-aligned in the template so that corner is free. The next
     control's `margin-inline-start: auto`, which spreads the pair across a
     full-width bar, is cleared so they group together. */
  @media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
    .shopify-section[id$='__product_reviews'] .carousel-content slideshow-arrows {
      inset: auto var(--util-page-margin-offset) 100% auto;
      padding-inline: 0;
      padding-block-end: 14px;
      gap: 8px;
    }

    .shopify-section[id$='__product_reviews'] .carousel-content .slideshow-control--next {
      margin-inline-start: 0;
    }
  }

  /* Phones keep Horizon's edge placement, just smaller. The controls are
     normally sized to --minimum-touch-target, 44px; these are brought down to
     36 so they sit lightly on a single card instead of dominating it. The
     theme's horizontal padding is dropped too, otherwise the circle grows wider
     than it is tall and the aspect-ratio on the shape fights it. */
  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .shopify-section[id$='__product_reviews'] .carousel-content .slideshow-control {
      min-inline-size: 36px;
      min-block-size: 36px;
      inline-size: 36px;
      block-size: 36px;
      padding: 0;
    }

    /* Hold the card clear of the controls instead of letting them sit on it.

       snippets/slideshow-styles.liquid implements the gutters as padding-inline
       on the track, and a slide is sized at 100% of that track's content box,
       so widening the gutter narrows and insets the card in one move - no
       margins to double up between slides, and scroll-padding-inline-start
       keeps the snap position right.

       The extra 44px is the 36px control plus an 8px gap, which leaves the card
       sitting exactly between the two arrows. --gutter-slide-width arrives as an
       inline custom property on an ancestor, so it is re-declared here on the
       track itself, where a directly set value beats an inherited one. */
    .shopify-section[id$='__product_reviews'] .carousel-content slideshow-slides {
      --gutter-slide-width: calc(var(--util-page-margin-offset) + 44px);
    }
  }

{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: footer link affordances.

  base.css only gives links a hover treatment when they sit inside a heading or
  a paragraph - `:is(h1..h6, p) > a:hover` and the `.rte` rule below it. Footer
  menu links are list items, and the utilities row is buttons and bare anchors,
  so none of them reacted to the pointer at all: no colour change, no underline,
  nothing to confirm the target before clicking.

  The hover darkens to the same navy as the column labels and draws an
  underline, so a link resolves towards the heading above it.

  --color-foreground would have been the obvious target, but it does not work
  here: each menu column sets its own text colour, and contrast-override
  redefines --color-foreground inside that block to the very colour the links
  already are - so the hover resolved to the resting colour and nothing moved.

  text-decoration-line is set explicitly rather than relying on the anchor's
  default underline being revealed by a colour change, which did not take
  effect on these list links.

  :focus-visible is matched alongside :hover so keyboard users get the same
  signal, which the theme's own rules also omit here.

  The links are named individually rather than excluding the social icons with
  `a:not(.social-icons__wrapper a)`. One invalid selector invalidates an entire
  comma-separated rule, and that form silently dropped the whole set - the
  policy links and the copyright link stopped reacting too. Listing the three
  real targets avoids relying on it, and keeps the social icons out of the
  colour change without a negation at all.

  Social icons are excluded from the colour change - they carry their platform
  colours - and lift slightly instead, which is dropped under reduced motion.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  /* One place to change the footer's ink. Brand navy is the palette's color1,
     but the only CSS variable carrying it is --color-primary-button-background,
     and binding footer text to a button token would mean restyling the buttons
     silently restyles the footer. A named variable keeps the intent obvious and
     the value in a single line. */
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'],
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] {
    --tm-footer-ink: #0b1f33;
  }

  /* Column labels in navy against the muted links beneath them, so each column
     reads as a heading with a list rather than one run of grey. The menu block
     has a single colour setting covering the heading and its links, so the two
     cannot be separated through settings alone. A declaration on the heading
     beats the colour the block inherits from its own .color-custom rule. */
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .menu__heading {
    color: var(--tm-footer-ink);
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .menu__item a,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .utilities a,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .utilities button {
    transition: color var(--animation-speed) var(--animation-easing),
      text-decoration-color var(--animation-speed) var(--animation-easing);
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .menu__item a:hover,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .menu__item a:focus-visible,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] a.footer-utilities__text:hover,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] a.footer-utilities__text:focus-visible,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .footer-utilities__group-copyright a:hover,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .footer-utilities__group-copyright a:focus-visible,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .policy-list-trigger:hover,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .policy-list-trigger:focus-visible {
    color: var(--tm-footer-ink);
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-color: currentcolor;
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a {
    display: inline-flex;
    transition: transform var(--animation-speed) var(--animation-easing),
      opacity var(--animation-speed) var(--animation-easing);
  }

  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a:hover,
  .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a:focus-visible {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    opacity: var(--opacity-80, 0.8);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_main'] .menu__item a,
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .utilities a,
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .utilities button {
      transition: none;
    }

    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a:hover,
    .shopify-section[id$='__footer_utilities'] .social-icons__wrapper a:focus-visible {
      transform: none;
    }
  }
{% endstyle %}

{% comment %}
  TrendMarkaz: About page, centred on phones.

  Scoped through the data-template attribute that layout/theme.liquid already
  writes on <main>, rather than a new class or the section keys. It is exact -
  only the about template matches - and because it sits on the main element the
  header and footer are untouched, which a body-level hook would not have been.

  Text blocks at full width have their alignment written into --text-align by
  snippets/text.liquid, and base.css applies that, so an inherited text-align
  from a centred ancestor loses. The variable is re-pointed and text-align set
  alongside it, the same pairing the footer brand column needed.

  The children are matched as well as the block. base.css aligns the heading or
  paragraph inside a text block directly, not only its wrapper, so centring the
  wrapper alone left the text itself still starting at the left edge.

  align-items on the flex columns is what moves the icons and the buttons; text
  alignment alone would leave them hanging at the left edge of a centred block.

  Desktop keeps the left alignment set per block in the template, so this is a
  phone-only treatment rather than a change to the page's design.
{% endcomment %}
{% style %}
  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    #MainContent[data-template='page.about'] .text-block,
    #MainContent[data-template='page.about'] .text-block > * {
      --text-align: center;
      text-align: center;
    }

    #MainContent[data-template='page.about'] .group-block-content,
    #MainContent[data-template='page.about'] .section-content-wrapper {
      align-items: center;
    }
  }
{% endstyle %}
